From 7ea92ceb77660671aa0194956fbb28ea1885ae4e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sunrisepeak <speakshen@163.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 21:31:02 +0900
Subject: [clangd] Module inputs are checked by where they come from, and kept
 across sessions

A memoized dependency scan and a BMI's validation verdict are reused only
while every file they read is as it was, and checking that is a stat or an
open per file. A global module fragment that includes a platform or framework
header reads hundreds of them, on every keystroke and every completion; on
Windows, where a stat opens a handle and resolves its final path, that is most
of what a completion costs.

Inputs are now checked by where they come from:

- Under a store given with --managed-input-root, a file belongs to a package
  version, <store>/<package>/<version>/, which only its package manager
  changes, rewriting the version's markers when it does. A scan or verdict
  records the markers' identity when it first reads from the version, and a
  later check compares the markers instead of the version's files.
- Under a system root of the command (-isystem, -idirafter, the sysroot, the
  toolchain the driver belongs to), an input is replayed once per session,
  as clang's own module input validation skips system inputs.
- Everything else is checked every time, as before.

The validation cache checks its inputs outside its lock, so one file's check
does not hold up another's.

With --module-memo-dir, the verdicts and the scans are written to a directory
while the process runs and read back by the next session, which otherwise
re-hashes every input of every BMI it reuses and preprocesses every module
unit again. What is read back is only a candidate: a verdict is used after its
inputs are checked, a scan after its manifest is replayed as on publication.
A kept scan names only the environment variables a driver reads for headers,
macros and tools, since the complete environment changes from one session to
the next.
